Docker Fundamentals
Docker – a platform that packages an application with everything it needs (code, runtime, libraries, system tools, settings) into a standardized unit called a container, so it runs identically anywhere (laptop, test server, cloud).
Dockerfile – a text file containing step-by-step instructions to build a Docker image (e.g. FROM, COPY, RUN, EXPOSE, CMD). Think of it as the "recipe."
Docker Image – a read-only, immutable template built from a Dockerfile. It contains the app code + dependencies + OS libraries, layered for efficiency. Think of it as the "class."
Docker Container – a running instance of an image, with its own isolated filesystem, network, and process space, plus a writable layer on top. Think of it as the "object" (instance of the class).
Flow: Dockerfile → (docker build) → Image → (docker run) → Container

Docker vs VM
Docker (Container)Virtual MachineVirtualizesOS-level (shares host kernel)Hardware (via hypervisor)OSNo guest OS — uses host kernelFull guest OS per VMSizeMBsGBsStartup timeSecondsMinutesIsolationProcess-level (weaker)Full OS-level (stronger)Resource overheadLowHighPortabilityVery highModerateUse caseMicroservices, CI/CD, fast scalingFull OS isolation, legacy apps, multi-OS needs
In short: VMs virtualize a whole computer; containers virtualize just the app layer, sharing the host's kernel — that's why they're so much lighter and faster.

Using Docker in a Real Project

Local development – Write a Dockerfile per service; use docker-compose.yml to spin up the whole stack (app + DB + cache) with one command, so every dev has an identical environment.
Version control for images – Tag images with commit SHA or semantic version (myapp:1.2.0), never rely on latest in production.
CI/CD pipeline – On each merge: build image → run unit/integration tests inside a container → push image to a registry (ECR/Docker Hub) → trigger deployment.
Registry – Store built images in a private registry (e.g., AWS ECR) so orchestration platforms can pull them.
Orchestration/deployment – Deploy containers via ECS, EKS (Kubernetes), or Docker Swarm; define scaling rules, health checks, rolling updates.
Microservices – Each service (auth, orders, payments) gets its own image/container, independently deployable and scalable.
Config & secrets – Inject environment variables/secrets at runtime (not baked into the image) via ECS task definitions or Kubernetes secrets.
Monitoring – Centralize logs/metrics from containers (CloudWatch, Prometheus/Grafana).


AWS Services and Real-Project Usage

EC2 – Resizable virtual servers. Use for hosting apps/backends that need full OS control; combine with Auto Scaling Groups + an Application Load Balancer for elastic web tiers.
ECS (Elastic Container Service) – Orchestrates and runs Docker containers at scale (Fargate = serverless, no server management; EC2 launch type = you manage the instances). Define task definitions and services; used to run microservices in production.
ECR (Elastic Container Registry) – Private Docker image registry. CI/CD pipeline does docker push to ECR after build; ECS/EKS pulls from ECR to deploy.
RDS – Managed relational database (MySQL/PostgreSQL/etc.) with automated backups, patching, and Multi-AZ failover. Use for structured, transactional data needing ACID (e.g., orders, payments, users).
DocumentDB – Managed, MongoDB-compatible document database. Use for semi-structured JSON-like data (e.g., product catalogs with varying attributes).
DynamoDB – Fully managed, serverless NoSQL key-value/document store with single-digit-millisecond latency at any scale. Use for high-throughput, low-latency data like shopping carts, sessions, user preferences.
Lambda – Serverless functions triggered by events (API call, file upload, queue message). No server management, pay per invocation. Great for event-driven logic: image resizing, order-confirmation processing, API backends.
API Gateway – Managed front door for APIs; routes HTTP requests to Lambda/EC2/ECS, handles auth, throttling, request validation, and versioning.
Kinesis – Real-time streaming data ingestion/processing (clickstreams, logs, IoT, order events) at scale; often paired with Lambda or Kinesis Data Analytics/Firehose to process/store the stream.
IAM – Manages users, roles, and policies controlling who/what can access which AWS resources — apply least-privilege permissions everywhere (e.g., a Lambda's execution role only gets access to the specific DynamoDB table it needs).
SNS – Pub/Sub notification service; one message fanned out to many subscribers (email, SMS, Lambda, SQS) — e.g., broadcast "order placed" event to multiple downstream consumers.
SQS – Managed message queue that decouples producers and consumers (e.g., order service pushes to a queue, inventory service consumes asynchronously) — smooths out traffic spikes and adds resiliency.


