Client (web/mobile) hits CloudFront, which serves cached static assets from S3 and forwards /api/* calls to API Gateway.
API Gateway routes requests to four independent Lambda microservices — Users, Products, Cart, Orders — a clean serverless microservices split, no server management needed.
Each service owns its own datastore (database-per-service pattern):

Users → RDS (relational, needs consistency for accounts/auth data)
Products → DocumentDB (flexible schema for varying product attributes)
Cart → DynamoDB (low-latency key-value, high read/write throughput, TTL for expiring carts)
Orders → RDS (needs ACID transactions for financial/order integrity)


When an order is placed, the Orders service pushes a message to SQS instead of processing it inline — this decouples the request from slower downstream work (inventory checks, payment confirmation) and smooths out traffic spikes.
An Order worker Lambda consumes the queue and does the actual processing, then publishes to SNS, which fans the event out to email/SMS/push notification subscribers.

Services used but not drawn as boxes (cross-cutting, not really a "step" in the request path):

ECR stores container images if any service is deployed on ECS/Fargate instead of Lambda (common for the Order worker if it needs longer execution time or more compute than Lambda's limits allow).
EC2 could sit behind an ALB for any service you choose not to run serverless.
IAM governs every arrow in the diagram — each Lambda gets an execution role scoped only to the specific table/queue/topic it needs (least privilege).
Kinesis would tap the API Gateway/client layer to stream clickstream and order events into an analytics pipeline (S3 + Athena/Redshift) for business intelligence, separate from the transactional path shown.

